<description>
Draw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.

Input structure:
- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.
- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.

Rule:
- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.
- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).
- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.
- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.

Key insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "3x3 glyphs left of a vertical divider, plus a gray start marker on the right",
  "dst": "a single polyline in the left canvas starting at the shifted gray marker",
  "match_cond": "glyphs ordered by tile column (left→right), then tile row (top→bottom)",
  "transform": "remove divider, shift gray into the left canvas, then append one segment per glyph while moving downward; segment length+orientation are determined by glyph type (area 4→vertical len2; area 5→horizontal len3 right; area 6→horizontal len4 left; area 7→horizontal len2 left)"
}
</annotation>
</reference_puzzle>

